<h2>Catch uncertainty before the program runs</h2><p>TypeScript checks many assumptions about data and function inputs while code is being built. If a function expects an order date, a type can catch an unrelated value before it reaches production. Early feedback is especially helpful when several people work on an API and its user interface at the same time. The goal is not to annotate every character. The goal is to make important, change-prone boundaries clear enough that the editor and compiler can help the team reason about them.</p>
<h2>Model the domain instead of labeling values</h2><p>Union types can describe a workflow as a limited set of valid states. An order might be placed, paid, or shipped. If the state is represented as any string, a typo can silently create an imaginary fourth state. A precise model makes missing cases visible and lets the compiler check whether every valid state has a useful response. This also improves conversations during review: teammates can discuss the lifecycle in the code’s own vocabulary instead of reconstructing it from scattered conditions.</p>
<h2>Static types do not validate live data</h2><p>Type information is removed when the application runs. It cannot clean or validate data arriving from an API, uploaded file, queue message, or environment variable. Use a runtime schema at those boundaries, report invalid input safely, and only then pass trusted values into the application. This distinction prevents a common false sense of security: a value can satisfy a TypeScript annotation in source code while still having a completely different shape when it comes from the network.</p>
<h2>Move an existing project gradually</h2><p>A JavaScript application does not need a single large rewrite. New modules can use TypeScript while older areas are migrated in small steps. Strict settings may reveal many assumptions at first, so prioritize boundaries that change often or carry a high cost when they fail. Permanent use of <code>any</code> only hides the signal and leaves the same uncertainty in place. Prefer a small, honest type or a runtime check over a cast that claims the data is safe without proving it.</p>
<h2>Keep client and server contracts aligned</h2><p>Typing component properties and service responses helps prevent consumers from drifting away from a shared contract. Renaming a property becomes a reliable project-wide search. Still, sharing a type file does not guarantee that independently deployed client and server versions remain compatible. Use versioning, tolerant readers, and thoughtful rollout plans when either side can change before the other. Types help developers work with a contract; they do not replace an operational strategy for changing it.</p>
<h2>Judge the value by reduced uncertainty</h2><p>Measure TypeScript through fewer repeated mistakes, shorter reviews, and safer refactoring, not by the number of type declarations. If code is full of forced casts and <code>any</code>, the boundary or abstraction may be wrong. A small model that a new teammate can understand is often more useful than a clever type designed to encode every imaginable case. Introduce complexity only when it prevents a concrete class of errors and remains easy to use correctly.</p>
HTML,
