<h2>One framework, several kinds of page</h2><p>Next.js builds on React and adds routing, server rendering, and production conventions. A public product page may benefit from HTML that is ready before a search crawler or visitor runs client JavaScript. A private dashboard may depend more heavily on browser interactions. The useful part of the framework is being able to choose a rendering approach for each route, instead of forcing every screen into the same pattern. Start from the page’s purpose and audience, then decide which parts need to run on the server or in the browser.</p>
<h2>Draw a deliberate server and browser boundary</h2><p>With the App Router, server components can fetch data and prepare content close to its source. Client components are needed for browser state, event handlers, and interfaces that change in response to user input. Marking a large part of the tree as client-side without a reason sends more code to the browser. On the other hand, putting an interaction into a server component cannot make it respond to a click. Keep the client boundary near the first behavior that genuinely requires the browser.</p>
<h2>Routes need metadata and useful failure states</h2><p>A folder-based route structure can make shared layouts and page ownership easier to understand. Each route should produce a title, description, canonical URL, and structured data from its own content so unrelated pages do not inherit duplicate metadata. A missing route deserves a helpful not-found page with a path back to relevant content. Errors can also be contained around independent sections, allowing one optional tool to fail without taking down the whole page.</p>
<h2>Protect performance where it matters</h2><p>Large images and fonts can cost more than framework code. Serve the right image dimensions, reserve its space to prevent layout shifts, and lazy-load media below the fold. Prioritize the main visual when it is important to the first view, but avoid making multiple assets compete for the same high priority. Track bundle size and real-user performance after release. A score from a single development machine does not describe the experience of a visitor on a slower device or network.</p>
<h2>Give data a clear owner</h2><p>Fetch information at the nearest level that owns its lifecycle. Fetching the same record independently in several components can create duplicated work, while putting every request in one enormous parent makes unrelated parts tightly coupled. Shared layout and loading boundaries should have a clear purpose. Keep authentication, data access, display, and form behavior understandable instead of building one page component that owns all of them. A clear owner makes caching, error handling, and future changes easier to reason about.</p>
<h2>Decide whether the framework fits</h2><p>Next.js is a strong fit when a product already uses React and needs several routes, server rendering, or a unified frontend structure. A tiny internal tool or static page may be easier to ship with a simpler setup. Consider the team’s React knowledge, search requirements, hosting model, data flow, and deployment process together. A new framework does not make a product faster or easier to use on its own; the value comes from applying its features to real constraints.</p>
