<h2>Use conventions to make intent visible</h2><p>Laravel provides a clear path through routing, validation, controllers, queues, and views. Those conventions help a new teammate find where a request is handled. Keep controllers focused on coordinating work rather than accumulating every business rule. Move reusable domain behavior into a service or model method when it has a real owner, and keep the route list readable. Framework convenience is most useful when the team can still explain what happens from request to response.</p>
<h2>Let Eloquent express the data relationships</h2><p>Eloquent makes common relationships and persistence straightforward, but query behavior still deserves attention. Eager-load relationships used by a page to avoid the N+1 pattern, and select only fields the view needs on large lists. A model accessor should clarify a stable concept rather than hide an expensive query. For bulk actions, measure the actual database work and use transactions when several writes must succeed or fail together. The ORM is a tool for expressing data access, not a reason to stop inspecting SQL.</p>
<h2>Validate input and authorize actions separately</h2><p>Validation answers whether submitted data has an acceptable shape. Authorization answers whether this person may perform this action on this record. Both checks are necessary, and hiding a button in the interface is not an access control. Use policies or gates at the server boundary, validate uploaded files by their real content and allowed size, and avoid exposing internal exception messages. For state-changing actions, consider CSRF protection, rate limits, and idempotency where a retry could create duplicate work.</p>
<h2>Make migrations safe to deploy</h2><p>Schema changes should work with the application version that is currently live and the one being released. Add a nullable column before requiring it, backfill data in a controlled step, then enforce the constraint after callers have changed. Large data updates may need a queued process instead of a long migration lock. For editorial records created by a seeder, use a stable unique key so repeated deployment does not make duplicates. Do not overwrite content a site editor has revised unless the workflow explicitly treats code as the source of truth.</p>
<h2>Use queues and caches with clear rules</h2><p>Queues are useful for work that does not need to hold open a web request, such as image processing or a reliable notification. A queued job needs retry limits, failure visibility, and idempotent behavior when possible. Caching can reduce repeated queries, but its key must include language or permission when those affect the result. Decide what invalidates the cache and how quickly an edit should appear. Measure first; a cache with unclear ownership can serve stale or private data longer than intended.</p>
<h2>Make release steps predictable</h2><p>Keep dependencies locked and test the application using the PHP version and extensions present in production. A deployment should clearly sequence code, assets, migrations, and cache rebuilds. Protect secrets in the environment, keep debug output disabled in production, and ensure writable storage belongs to the application user. Test a rollback on a safe copy of data. Reliable releases are part of the product: a feature that cannot be deployed without risking customer content is not ready.</p>
