<h2>Start with the interaction you need</h2><p>Vue is designed to make reactive interfaces approachable, from a small interactive area on an existing page to a larger application with routes and shared state. Before choosing a structure, describe what changes when a person uses the page. A search box, checkout flow, and reporting dashboard each have different state and loading needs. Avoid adding a complex global layer to a screen whose data belongs to a single component. Structure should follow the product’s interaction and the team’s likely changes.</p>
<h2>Give components a clear contract</h2><p>A component is easier to reuse when its inputs, events, and responsibilities are explicit. Keep one component focused on one meaningful part of the interface and pass only the data it needs. If several nested components need the same value, a parent owner or a small store may help. Do not move every value into shared state by default; global state makes unrelated screens depend on each other and can make updates harder to trace. Prefer a computed value when data can be derived instead of stored twice.</p>
<h2>Use reactivity deliberately</h2><p>Watchers are useful when a change must trigger an external effect, such as synchronizing a query parameter or starting a request. If a value can be calculated from existing state, a computed property is usually simpler. Too many watchers can create feedback loops and surprising order. For asynchronous requests, cancel or disregard stale results when the search term or route changes. Otherwise, a slow earlier request can overwrite the answer to the user’s latest action.</p>
<h2>Plan form and route behavior</h2><p>Long forms need saved progress, clear validation, and a way to leave without losing important work. Keep error messages near their fields and associate them so assistive technology can announce them. Put shareable filters in the URL so people can return to the same view. When a route changes, update the document title and place keyboard focus where the new page begins. These details help all users understand that navigation has happened rather than leaving them in an apparently unchanged interface.</p>
<h2>Grow a shared library with evidence</h2><p>For components used across pages, document normal, loading, empty, error, and disabled states. Check long copy, narrow screens, language direction, and larger text before publishing the pattern. Use design tokens from one source so nearby values do not drift across teams. A large component library is not a goal by itself: begin with repeated needs and clear owners. Review adoption and remove outdated patterns so the library reflects the product people actually use.</p>
<h2>Test the experience, not framework details</h2><p>Unit tests can cover transformations and reusable logic; browser tests should focus on high-value journeys such as completing a form or changing a filter. Assert what the user sees and can do rather than Vue’s internal component tree. Include keyboard and touch behavior, plus network failure where it matters. A well-designed Vue application makes state transitions visible, keeps component ownership understandable, and gives the team a safe path to add features without rebuilding its structure each time.</p>
