<h2>A compiler-centered way to build UI</h2><p>Svelte lets developers describe components in a concise format and compiles much of the framework work into browser code during the build. This can make interactions feel direct and keep runtime overhead modest. A compiler does not automatically guarantee a small or fast product, however. A large editor, charting package, or image can outweigh the framework itself. Evaluate the output that ships, not only the source code that looks simple in the repository.</p>
<h2>Choose state ownership carefully</h2><p>Keep state close to the components that own it. A local input value rarely needs a global store, while a user session or cart shared across routes may. Derived values should be computed from their sources instead of copied into another variable that can become stale. When a component changes or disappears, understand which subscriptions, timers, and requests remain active. Clear ownership makes a Svelte interface easier to debug because the team can follow an update back to the action that caused it.</p>
<h2>Handle asynchronous work as real state</h2><p>Requests can be pending, successful, empty, or failed. Represent these cases intentionally rather than showing a blank panel until data arrives. If a search query changes quickly, cancel an earlier request or ignore its response after a newer request starts. Otherwise, a delayed answer can replace current results with stale ones. For a long action, show progress and a useful recovery option. A graceful interface tells the person whether they should wait, correct input, or retry.</p>
<h2>Keep server and browser responsibilities distinct</h2><p>SvelteKit can render content on the server and add client behavior where needed. Separate data that is safe for public HTML from private values that must stay on the server. A public environment variable is visible to the browser, regardless of its name. Decide how each route behaves on direct load, refresh, back navigation, and missing data. Server rendering is useful for content discovery and first paint, but metadata, canonical links, and meaningful page content still need to be correct.</p>
<h2>Review output and dependencies</h2><p>After a build, inspect chunk sizes, duplicate dependencies, and any source maps that should not be public. Compiler optimizations remove some runtime work; they cannot remove the cost of an unnecessary library. Lock the runtime and package manager versions in CI so a different machine does not produce a different result. Track bundle changes when adding a feature that can affect the initial route. A compact application should remain compact as it grows.</p>
<h2>Build accessible behavior into components</h2><p>Use semantic elements, keyboard support, visible focus, and concise labels. Error messages should be announced and tied to the corresponding fields. For transitions, account for reduced-motion preferences and keep essential content available when animation is disabled. Test a narrow viewport and both left-to-right and right-to-left copy when the product serves more than one language. Svelte makes it quick to build a component; reliable behavior still comes from thoughtful design and direct testing.</p>
