<figure><img src="https://cdn.simpleicons.org/flutter/02569b" alt="Flutter logo" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The Flutter mark, featured in this Flowsee engineering guide.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>One codebase does not mean one experience</h2><p>Flutter lets a team build interfaces for several platforms from a shared codebase. Shared widgets and product logic can reduce duplication, but users still expect each platform to feel familiar. Keyboard behavior, navigation, permissions, text scaling, and system conventions vary. Define which behaviors should be shared and where a native adaptation improves the experience. A successful cross-platform plan preserves a coherent product while respecting the device people are using.</p>
<h2>Compose widgets around product responsibilities</h2><p>Flutter’s widget model encourages composition. Keep a widget focused on one visual or behavioral responsibility, and pass the data it needs through clear parameters. If state is copied into several places, those copies can disagree after a change. Decide whether state belongs to one screen, a feature, or the whole application. Use a state management package when it solves a real ownership problem, not just because it is popular. Simple structure often scales better than a global pattern applied everywhere.</p>
<h2>Design for real screens and real content</h2><p>A layout should adapt to narrow and wide screens, larger text settings, and content that takes more space than a mockup. Avoid fixed dimensions where text can grow or translations can wrap. Reserve space for images and load the right resolution for the device. Check contrast, touch target size, and semantics for assistive technology. If an interface supports Persian and English, test direction, icon placement, and mixed-language labels instead of assuming a mirrored layout will always be correct.</p>
<h2>Make native integrations explicit</h2><p>Plugins can connect Flutter to cameras, notifications, files, maps, and platform services. Check the plugin’s maintenance, platform coverage, permissions, and behavior when a capability is unavailable. Treat incoming data from a platform channel like any other external input. Give the user a useful fallback if permission is denied or a plugin is not supported on a target. A feature should fail in a clear, recoverable way rather than opening an empty panel.</p>
<h2>Test the important paths on devices</h2><p>Widget tests are fast for isolated rendering and logic; integration tests can cover essential user journeys. Still, real device checks catch issues that simulators and mocks may miss, such as keyboard resizing, permission dialogs, and platform-specific scrolling. Include slow network and offline scenarios when data matters. Report crashes with app version, device, and operating system while avoiding unnecessary personal identifiers. A test plan should cover each supported platform, not only the one used by the main developer.</p>
<h2>Own releases separately for each platform</h2><p>Store review, signing, package versions, and system permissions follow different rules across Android, iOS, and web. Maintain a capability matrix so the team knows what each target supports. Upgrade Flutter and plugins on a regular schedule, and trial changes before a major release. For offline data, show the last sync time and a retry option. If local and remote changes conflict, communicate the policy rather than silently choosing a version that could discard a person’s work.</p>
