<figure><img src="https://cdn.simpleicons.org/react/61dafb" alt="React logo" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The React mark, featured in this Flowsee engineering guide.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>Think in reusable pieces of interface</h2><p>React builds a user interface from components that receive data and describe what should appear. A useful component owns one responsibility, has a clear contract, and can be understood without reading the whole application. Avoid extracting every line into a component; reuse is valuable when it reflects a shared behavior or a clear boundary. Keep business decisions separate enough that they can be tested without depending on how a particular button or card is rendered.</p>
<h2>Put state where it belongs</h2><p>Local state is a good fit for an open menu or an input that only one component uses. Shared state may belong higher in the tree or in a store when several routes rely on the same value. Duplicating state creates synchronization work: two copies can disagree after one changes. Prefer deriving values from their source instead of storing both the source and a second representation. A component tree is easier to maintain when every piece of important state has an obvious owner.</p>
<h2>Keep rendering predictable</h2><p>State and props should describe the current screen. Avoid changing data during render, since that can lead to repeated work and confusing results. Effects are for synchronizing with systems outside React, such as a browser event listener or network request; they are not a default place for every calculation. If a derived value can be computed during render, an extra state variable and effect may introduce an unnecessary delay or stale intermediate state.</p>
<h2>Treat asynchronous states as part of the design</h2><p>A data-driven screen can be loading, empty, successful, refreshing, or failed. Model these states explicitly so the interface does not leave users staring at a blank area. When the selected account or route changes, an earlier network response should not overwrite the latest result. Abort requests when possible or guard against stale responses. An error boundary can isolate rendering failures, but retry only helps when the underlying problem can actually change.</p>
<h2>Measure before optimizing re-renders</h2><p>React provides tools for profiling slow components, but memoizing every value can make code harder to follow without improving the experience. Profile the actual interaction and identify what renders too often or takes too long. Large lists may need virtualization; oversized bundles may need route-level splitting. Keep state local to reduce unnecessary updates across the tree. Use performance budgets that reflect real devices, and repeat the measurement after an optimization to verify it helped.</p>
<h2>Test user outcomes</h2><p>Test reusable logic quickly and use browser tests for important flows such as changing a filter, saving a form, or recovering from a failed request. Assertions should focus on what a user can see and do rather than internal component structure. Include keyboard navigation, focus after route changes, and screen reader labels. Good tests make a refactor safer while leaving the implementation free to evolve. React is most effective when its component boundaries match the way the product’s behavior is understood.</p>
