<figure><img src="https://cdn.simpleicons.org/php/777bb4" alt="PHP logo" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The PHP mark, featured in this Flowsee engineering guide.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>Build on the modern language</h2><p>PHP has grown from a simple scripting language into a capable platform for long-running web applications and APIs. Namespaces, typed properties, enums, and improved error behavior help teams express intent more clearly. Use types to make important contracts visible, but keep abstractions grounded in real product needs. A clean architecture is not a collection of layers by itself; it is a set of boundaries that lets the team change business rules without touching unrelated delivery details.</p>
<h2>Make Composer part of the application contract</h2><p>Composer resolves packages and records the installed dependency versions in a lock file. Commit that lock file for applications so production and development install the same dependency graph. Keep packages updated on a deliberate schedule and review security advisories. Avoid adding a dependency for a small behavior that the standard library already handles well. The size and maintenance of the dependency tree are product costs, so each package should have a clear job and an owner.</p>
<h2>Secure requests at the boundary</h2><p>Validate request shape, size, and allowed values before data reaches domain logic. Escape output for the context where it is rendered, use prepared database queries, and protect state-changing browser forms against cross-site request forgery. Authentication and authorization are different: a signed-in user still needs permission to act on a particular resource. Treat uploaded files, webhook bodies, and environment values as external input, and never expose raw exception details or secrets in a public response.</p>
<h2>Keep operations resilient</h2><p>Requests to external services need finite timeouts. Retries should be bounded and safe to repeat; otherwise an outage can multiply work or create duplicate actions. For payments or order creation, use an idempotency mechanism so the same intent is not applied twice. Queue work that does not need to finish before the HTTP response, and make failed jobs visible to operators. A useful error response should provide a stable status and a correlation ID without revealing private information.</p>
<h2>Plan runtime upgrades carefully</h2><p>Supported PHP versions and extensions change, so track the runtime required by the current framework and dependencies. Run deprecation warnings in development and resolve them before the next upgrade makes them fatal. Test the exact Composer lock file against the PHP version and operating system used in production. A release process should make configuration, migrations, cache warming, and rollback behavior explicit. Debug output should be disabled in production, and logs should be private and limited to the details needed for diagnosis.</p>
<h2>Measure the service people use</h2><p>Monitor response time, error rate, memory use, queue delay, and database pressure together. One metric rarely identifies the cause of a slowdown. Profile a real request before optimizing, then confirm the change under the same conditions. Keep tests around critical workflows such as registration and checkout, and use a copy of production-like data for migration rehearsals. A maintainable PHP application is one the team can update securely without losing user data or relying on a single developer’s memory.</p>
