        'title' => 'Electron: Building Desktop Applications with Web Technologies',
        'excerpt' => 'Understand Electron’s main and renderer processes, secure IPC, packaging, updates, and the trade-offs of desktop web applications.',
        'cover_alt' => 'Electron logo and development guide',
        'content' => <<<'HTML'
<figure><img src="https://cdn.simpleicons.org/electron/47848f" alt="Electron logo" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The Electron mark, featured in this Flowsee engineering guide.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>Bring web skills to a desktop product</h2><p>Electron combines Chromium with Node.js so a team can build desktop software using web technologies. Sharing frontend skills can shorten development time, and a desktop app can offer local files, menus, and operating system integrations. The trade-off is a larger install and a second set of release responsibilities. Decide whether desktop capabilities solve a real user problem. If the product only needs a web page, packaging a browser may add update and security work without adding meaningful value.</p>
<h2>Separate the main and renderer processes</h2><p>The main process owns application windows and privileged operating-system work. A renderer displays the user interface and should receive only the capabilities it needs. Keep Node integration disabled in untrusted renderer content, enable context isolation, and expose a small preload API rather than the full runtime. This boundary limits the damage if content is compromised. Treat every message crossing the boundary as untrusted input, even when it comes from a UI component maintained by your team.</p>
<h2>Make IPC narrow and auditable</h2><p>Inter-process communication should offer specific actions with validated arguments, such as opening a selected file or saving a known document format. Avoid a generic method that lets the renderer invoke arbitrary operating-system commands. Validate file paths, enforce allowed origins, and return structured errors that the interface can display safely. For external links, use the user’s default browser and a strict protocol allowlist. Do not load a remote URL into a window that has access to privileged preload functions.</p>
<h2>Protect updates and local data</h2><p>Application updates should come from a trusted source and be verified according to the release platform. Keep secrets out of the renderer and do not store credentials in plain text. Local files may contain private work, so explain what is saved and provide a recovery path where appropriate. Back up or migrate local data carefully across application versions. A new release should not silently make an older file unusable, and a failed update should not leave the person with a broken installation and no clear way back.</p>
<h2>Design the lifecycle and release process</h2><p>Define what happens when a window closes during a save, how the process exits, and whether reopening restores the prior workspace. Clean shutdown should close listeners and background tasks so a second launch does not inherit orphaned processes. Packaging, code signing, store review, and automatic updates differ across operating systems. Test signed builds on each supported platform, not only a development build on one machine. Keep crash reports tied to app version and platform while avoiding unnecessary personal information.</p>
<h2>Choose Electron with eyes open</h2><p>Electron is a strong choice when teams need cross-platform desktop behavior and can share a meaningful amount of web code. It may be too heavy for a small utility that can run in the browser, or a poor fit when native performance and system integration are central. Compare installation size, memory use, update ownership, security review, and the user’s working environment. A desktop wrapper is a product commitment: it brings the operating system into your support and release plan.</p>
