<h2>Start the file with a product question</h2><p>Figma is more useful when it records product decisions than when it only contains polished screens. Before drawing a page, clarify who will use the experience, what they need to accomplish, and what success looks like. Map the main journey and create a low-detail version to expose unclear steps early. List important roles and states so the design does not describe only an optimistic path. A clear question keeps exploration focused and helps the team tell the difference between a visual preference and a real usability problem.</p>
<h2>Organize pages for shared work</h2><p>Separate a file into understandable areas such as flows, the component library, and screens ready for review. Name frames by page and state instead of leaving a trail of duplicates called “copy 2.” For a major change, add a short note about why it was made. That context helps the next designer understand which decisions are intentional and which details are temporary. A shared design file should be navigable by a teammate who was not in the original meeting.</p>
<h2>Keep the design system small and dependable</h2><p>Record repeated colors, text styles, spacing, and corner values as variables or tokens so consistency does not rely on memory. Name tokens by purpose, such as “text primary,” rather than by a value that may change with a new theme. A large library is unnecessary at the beginning. Document a few repeated patterns and expand after the team sees how they are used. Give each component and token a clear owner, and periodically remove unused entries so the library remains easy to browse.</p>
<h2>Prototype to answer a specific question</h2><p>A prototype is most valuable when it tests an uncertainty: can someone find the next step, understand a form error, or recover from a mistake? Define the starting point, expected result, and alternate states for each important interaction. Connecting every button to another screen may look clickable but does not necessarily test a realistic flow. Ask representative users to complete a concrete task, then observe where they pause or make an unexpected choice. Behavioral evidence is more useful than a general question about whether the screen looks nice.</p>
<h2>Review accessibility before handoff</h2><p>Check text contrast, target sizes, reading order, and whether meaning depends on color alone. Add a label or icon to communicate success and errors. Test a narrow mobile frame with long content and larger text rather than an empty artboard. If the product supports Persian and English, check writing direction, text length, and icon placement in both. Recording accessibility notes beside common components makes repeated decisions more reliable and helps developers understand which behavior must be preserved.</p>
<h2>Hand off context, then review the result</h2><p>Document responsive states, measurements, error behavior, spacing, and asset names beside the design. Identify the intended image crop and aspect ratio, and avoid sending several ambiguous copies of the same file. Dev Mode can expose measurements, but it cannot explain data behavior, loading, or product rules. A short walkthrough can surface risky assumptions before implementation. After development, compare the live result with the design and record meaningful differences. Keeping design and code in conversation prevents the two from drifting apart.</p>
